    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In a DC circuit, a capacitor acts like a short circuit, allowing current to flow through it. In contrast, in an AC circuit, a capacitor acts like an open circuit, opposing the change in voltage and thus blocking the flow of current. This is because the capacitor stores charge and resists changes in voltage, which is less of an issue in DC where voltage remains constant.\n\nThe formula for capacitive reactance (Xc) is Xc = 1 / (2 * π * f * C), where f is the frequency of the AC signal and C is the capacitance of the capacitor.",
